<p><code>sumFunc1</code> and <code>sumFunc2</code> are functions that sum t=
wo numbers. They've been created by the <code>factory()</code> function.</p=
>
<p>The functions <code>sumFunc1</code> and <code>sumFunc2</code> share the =
same code source, but they are different function objects. Comparing them <=
code>sumFunc1 =3D=3D=3D sumFunc2</code> evaluates to <code>false</code>.</p=
>
<p>That's just how JavaScript objects work. An object (including a function=
 object) <a href=3D"https://dmitripavlutin.com/the-legend-of-javascript-equ=
ality-operator/#the-identity-operator">equals</a> only to itself.</p>
