        <div class=3D"markdown-body markdown-body-scrollable">
          <p dir=3D"auto">@alisher-usmonov I'm not sure why you got the rea=
ction you did, rather than someone helping to answer your question. Many pe=
ople have this same question at first.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">The main difference is that <code class=3D"notranslate">use=
Memo</code> is used for values, and <code class=3D"notranslate">useCallback=
</code> is used for functions.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">Here's a contrived example:</p>
<div class=3D"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overf=
low-auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=3D"const someValue =3D useMem=
o(() =3D> someLongList.reduce((acc, item) =3D> acc + item.value), [someLong=
List]);"><pre class=3D"notranslate"><code class=3D"notranslate">const someV=
alue =3D useMemo(() =3D&gt; someLongList.reduce((acc, item) =3D&gt; acc + i=
tem.value), [someLongList]);
</code></pre></div>
<p dir=3D"auto">Here we have <code class=3D"notranslate">someLongList</code=
> that we need to sum up values for. We don't want to run this on each rend=
er since the list is long and that would make this an expensive calculation=
. With <code class=3D"notranslate">useMemo</code>, we can memoize the value=
, and only re-calculate when <code class=3D"notranslate">someLongList</code=
> changes</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">For useCallback</p>
<div class=3D"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overf=
low-auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=3D"const someHandlerFunction =
=3D useCallback(() =3D> { ... }, []);"><pre class=3D"notranslate"><code cla=
ss=3D"notranslate">const someHandlerFunction =3D useCallback(() =3D&gt; { .=
.. }, []);
</code></pre></div>
<p dir=3D"auto">Here we have a function we've memoized. See the original ar=
